@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+@captain @conformance
+Feature: Editing hides the non-edit controls
+
+  When a task enters editing mode the app adds the "editing" class to its item
+  element and the non-edit controls (the checkbox, label and destroy button
+  inside ".view") must become not visible. A real browser applies the
+  stylesheet rule that hides ".view" for an ".editing" item; without that rule
+  the controls stay visible and the editing assertion fails. The in-harness DOM
+  does not compute stylesheet visibility, so the product behaviour must be
+  pinned by a source-level check on the shipped stylesheets.
+
+  Scenario: Editing mode hides the non-edit controls
+    Given the implementation directories from RIGGING.md
+    When the editing-controls-hidden conformance check inspects the shipped stylesheets
+    Then an item in editing mode hides its non-edit controls through a stylesheet rule
diff --git a/features/conformance/item-element-identity.feature b/features/conformance/item-element-identity.feature
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..02dcd3f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/features/conformance/item-element-identity.feature
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+@captain @conformance
+Feature: Item element identity preservation
+
+  A real browser holds references to the item elements that the app has
+  rendered. When a task's completed state changes, the app must mutate the
+  existing item element in place: toggle its "completed" class and its
+  checkbox, and reflect the mark-all control, without discarding and rebuilding
+  the rendered item list. Rebuilding the list detaches every item element the
+  browser already references, so an interaction that spans the change fails
+  with a detached-from-DOM error. The in-harness DOM re-queries after each
+  step, so it cannot see this defect; only a source-level check can pin it.
+
+  Scenario: A single item toggle preserves the item element instance
+    Given the implementation directories from RIGGING.md
+    When the item-element-identity conformance check inspects the single-item toggle path
+    Then toggling one item preserves the item element instance the browser already references
+
+  Scenario: The mark-all toggle preserves every item element instance
+    Given the implementation directories from RIGGING.md
+    When the item-element-identity conformance check inspects the mark-all toggle path
+    Then toggling all items preserves every item element instance the browser already references
